ment to decreased plasma osmolality due to abnormal vasopressin secretion, potentially leading to water intoxication [Data: Relationships (1179, 2122)]. Thus, SIADH serves as a critical intermediary between carbamazepine exposure and systemic physiological disruption.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"卡马西平 directly affects key clinical indicators, particularly血浆渗透压\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Beyond causing toxicity through overdose or drug interactions, carbamazepine itself influences important laboratory indicators. Most notably, it is associated with reduced血浆渗透压, an effect attributed to its induction of抗利尿激素异常分泌综合征. Multiple sources confirm that carbamazepine therapy leads to decreased plasma osmolality, along with reductions in serum sodium, potassium, and chloride [Data: Relationships (1179, 2122); Entities (13969)]. Water load tests in patients on carbamazepine show impaired free water clearance and reduced urinary excretion capacity, supporting the mechanism of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion [Data: Relationships (1179)]. Monitoring of plasma osmolality and electrolytes is therefore recommended during carbamazepine treatment, especially in the initial phase, to prevent complications such as hyponatremic encephalopathy [Data: Relationships (1179)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Drug interactions significantly elevate risk of carbamazepine toxicity\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Although not explicitly listed as entities in the provided data, the evidence within relationship descriptions repeatedly highlights that co-administration of carbamazepine with certain antibiotics—particularly erythromycin and midecamycin—precipitates toxicity by inhibiting carbamazepine metabolism. Erythromycin reduces carbamazepine clearance, leading to supratherapeutic plasma concentrations. Case reports describe patients developing full-blown toxicity (including嗜睡昏沉, 倦怠, 运动失调, 呕吐, and眼球震颤) within 2–3 days of startin